---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import NextSteps from '../../components/NextSteps.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---

<DocsLayout title="Overview">
  <h1>Start with the reduction loop.</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    The entry point is simple: you already have a large array of turns, and you do not want to keep sending that whole thing into the next model call.
    Nocturnus reduces the working set first, then helps you keep later turns lean.
  </p>

  <div class="callout callout-info">
    <strong>Default mental model.</strong> Your app owns the thread. Nocturnus owns the cut. That is the product story the docs are optimized around.
  </div>

  <h2>Start in four moves</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Send raw turns to <code>POST /context</code> (requires LLM — see note below).</li>
    <li>Narrow the next decision with <code>POST /memory/context</code> and <code>goals</code>.</li>
    <li>Reuse the same <code>sessionId</code> with <code>POST /context/diff</code>.</li>
    <li>Clear the session when the thread ends.</li>
  </ol>

  <pre data-lang="bash"><code>curl -X POST http://localhost:9300/context \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -H "X-Tenant-ID: default" \
  -d '&#123;
    "turns": [
      "User cannot log in after Okta cutover.",
      "CRM says enterprise tier and billing current.",
      "Audit log shows SAML issuer mismatch."
    ]
  &#125;'</code></pre>

  <div class="callout callout-warning">
    <strong>LLM extraction required for <code>POST /context</code>.</strong>
    This endpoint extracts structured facts from raw text using an LLM.
    Set <code>OPENAI_API_KEY</code>, <code>ANTHROPIC_API_KEY</code>, or configure <code>LLM_BASE_URL</code>/<code>LLM_MODEL</code> (e.g. Ollama).
    Without LLM config, start with <code>POST /tell</code> to assert facts directly, then use <code>POST /memory/context</code> — no LLM needed.
  </div>

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <div class="docs-card-grid">
    <div class="docs-card">
      <div class="docs-tag">Workflow</div>
      <h3>See the real loop</h3>
      <p>The context workflow page walks through first-pass reduction, goal-specific narrowing, and later-turn diffs.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="docs-card">
      <div class="docs-tag">Integration</div>
      <h3>Pick your surface</h3>
      <p>Use the API, SDKs, CLI, or MCP depending on where your app or agent runtime already lives.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="docs-card">
      <div class="docs-tag">Backend mechanics</div>
      <h3>Read this second</h3>
      <p>Predicates, rules, salience, and truth maintenance still matter, but they belong behind the reduction story, not in front of it.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="docs-card">
      <div class="docs-tag">Operations</div>
      <h3>Run it safely</h3>
      <p>Security, multi-tenancy, and operations remain documented, but they stay in the operating lane rather than the front-page lane.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <NextSteps links={[
    { title: 'Context Workflow', description: 'Walk through /context, /context/optimize, and /context/diff', href: `${base}/docs/context` },
    { title: 'API Reference', description: 'See every HTTP endpoint and request shape', href: `${base}/docs/api` },
    { title: 'SDKs and MCP', description: 'Pick the integration surface that matches your stack', href: `${base}/docs/sdks` },
    { title: 'How It Works', description: 'Read the backend mechanics after the workflow is clear', href: `${base}/docs/concepts` },
  ]} />
</DocsLayout>
